this is a mandatory process in china all  frozen seafood imports must be tested  for the coronavirus  after it was found on food packaging in  june last year  but the regulations are causing  congestion at ports and hundreds of  container ships are held up  in the north china i think somehow it's  more of a problem in north china  and less so in in shanghainengo and the  south  quite a lot of the cargos was diverted  to korean  ports some of the cargo's heading to the  north china  they have no choice then they directed  it to shanghai an outbreak in the  northeastern city of dalian last month  was linked to frozen food imports  dock workers who handled the goods  tested positive for covert 19  authorities blamed food packaging but  the world health organization  has questioned the link the reason that  some of those  food handlers  got infected is probably that they  did not practice any social distancing  measures the direct transmission of the  virus  from the contaminated food to human  beings that should be  very rare either way the restrictions  are causing long delays  and a shortage of imported food supplies  into china  the most immediate impact will be a  sudden imbalance of the flow  of the food supply you know into and  outside of china  china is still a country that is very  very very hungry for protein and china  despite  all the the natural resources is a huge  importer of great seafood from around  the world  the demand is quite high for the frozen  foods like  cherries from australia cherries from  chile  and also the frozen meat from brazil  here in hong kong the government has  followed china's lead and has now  extended testing to include frozen food  items  it's now testing a wider variety of food  items from around  30 countries the city's health chief  says the risk of infection from touching  the surface  of frozen packaging is minimal and so  far  no cases have been recorded the shortage  of port space  and risk of long delays has prompted  some companies  to cancel shipments to china but chinese  authorities  are showing no sign they'll ease up on  the regulations  sarah clark al jazeera hong kong
 

